-- generalized and beyond tremedously improved scrapping logic, now generalized to almost all if not all unitAIs (see known issue 52 in military (AI) section for details), in `CvUnit::canScrap()`. What this means is we now don't destroy our units and then rebuild them because we now think we don't have enough, causing us to be stuck in endless very inefficient loops. This is adjusted by unitAI types and now also has a matching logic that handles which unit to allow the production order of, with overproduction quotas in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit`, based on conditions such as war and such, for example we don't want explore units or settlers at war or danger, but we definitely want land units then. As for naval units, if map is naval heavy (e.g. archipelago, etc) we don't apply naval unit restrictions, as many strategies could work (a frigate defeating a cargo of invaders is as good as a stack of units fighting said invaders on land after they land), but much less naval units if not (e.g. pangea, continents, etc.), and still regardless of map type, we don't want these at war or danger so max is lower if not 0 then at production stage, but as for scrapping, don't scrap existing ones. Workers having a decaying logic for max units so we scrap excess as they are less relevant later if helps (i don't know if they cost maintenance xd but hopefully maybe helps (check if accurate)) quite conservatively. This fixes issue of AI in some cases dying in pangea in particular while overproducing despite our previous changes, in some cases still (most likely due to code that short circuited our previous changes or/and our changes not being effective or relevant enough maybe). AI is much more competitive/stronger now, and in same save file quickly shifted/adjustedits production to land units and survival mode when in excess naval units artifically from this desperate already too late save file, so hopefully starting from an earlier time mistake would not have been made. See for details [53 - (Beyond Tremendously Improved) Naval dementia of producing privateers/galleons then seemingly scrapping them and repeat, or/and of more importantly building galleons and privateers in droves and excess, despite enemy threatening cities of land capture for 20+ turns, and losing capital as a result](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#53---beyond-tremendously-improved-naval-dementia-of-producing-privateersgalleons-then-seemingly-scrapping-them-and-repeat-orand-of-more-importantly-building-galleons-and-privateers-in-droves-and-excess-if-i-may-say-but-anyways-etc-despite-enemy-threatening-cities-of-land-capture-for-20-turns-and-losing-capital-as-a-result-anyways-etc-fixedaddressed-by-now-managing-production-of-each-unitai-with-max-by-type-in-cvcityaiai_chooseunit-as-well-as-disallowing-scrapping-and-managing-it-by-unitai-type-globally-as-well-in-cvunitcanscrap-by-type-as-well-with-max-and-such-other-conditions-for-some-units-like-as-of-now-workers-anyways-etc). Hopefully we also handle with this possible other silent issues that may have happened like for the workboats infinite loop (see [23 - (Seemingly now fixed) Major bug of AI cities being stuck in a loop of producing a workboat and instantly scrapping it](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#23---seemingly-now-fixed-major-bug-of-ai-cities-being-stuck-in-a-loop-of-producing-a-workboat-and-instantly-scrapping-it-so-without-producing-a-new-unit-if-im-not-mistaken-then-producing-a-new-one-endlessly-for-dozen-turns-until-it-somehow-solved-itself-but-way-too-late) for details) (sometimes dozens of turns, and not a rare bug) or such other unknown issues possibly preemptively fixed, which hopefully help AI a lot not proceed with nonsensical or/and poorly coordinated logic it is fed/told.
-- before the no production (see below) fix/patch is applied, also added an emergency/force water food building class (as of now it is the harbor buildings in advciv-sas (buildings that give +1 food on water tiles)) logic that takes precedence: if we are low on food and city is coastal (connected to an ocean), force a harbor type of building (or their civ-specific variants) so that we don't get stagnant while a harbor could help us grow our pop.
-- add emergency defense buildings to build priority if we at a high enough risk of being attacked (as of now if enemy team is significantly stronger than ours, or if there is nearby danger, or if we are at war and enemy team is not significantly weaker than ours (risk of being backstabbed even if we are quite strong, so being more cautious in this case)), force build at highest priority walls and then castle in our city, if these are missing, in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. Trying not to overdo it as they are quite costly to build and may hinder/slow our growth, so these are applied in conservative cases where it may be most beneficial if i'm not mistaken as mentionned here. After this change, it seems snowballer AIs have a significantly harder time steamrolling and other players defend themselves better in autoplay (check if accurate). Update: now no longer applies if city is very low hammer (scales per era) or has mostly water tiles in its inner BFC, as this type of cities would be more efficiently used as economy cities rather (can't produce units fast enough or/and low likeliness to be attacked). This also helps not fire the no production unit fallback or emergency defense too soon where city could build other things rather than spend too much time on one of these things to build which are not key early and detrimental to city growth, and so which is a nice sanity. Update 2: this emergency-defense path is disabled for water-heavy cities (see related update 2 below).
-- massively patched / worked around and seemingly fixed the major base advciv +/- civ4 issue of AI cities being in a no production state (they don't produce anything at all, there is a "no entry" like button as chatgpt 5 describe it xd), not even a unit like a warrior / ancient maceman or such which would absolutely be free and better than litteral nothing. This wastes a lot of hammers, and happens in quite a few cities, not just during one turn. The very effective work around was to add another fallback of "produce most expensive (unless price is extremely and unusually high then cheapest among them instead) unit that suits us" (a land unit of offense or defense, not civilian like a scout for example). So now cities don't have no production at all anymore, and produce for example an archer instead in the ancient era as of now. Added in `CvCity::AI_doTurn`. See for details with screenshots and such [51 - (Worked around / fixed) Massive seemingly base advciv +/- civ4 issue if i'm not mistaken of many cities entering no production early for 1 or several turns many times during the game early (and possibly later this is why many cities have a process rather than no production, as processes are not available early and are listed among fallbacks if production fails it seems but check to be sure)](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#51---worked-around--fixed-massive-seemingly-base-advciv---civ4-issue-if-im-not-mistaken-of-many-cities-entering-no-production-early-for-1-or-several-turns-many-times-during-the-game-early-and-possibly-later-this-is-why-many-cities-have-a-process-rather-than-no-production-as-processes-are-not-available-early-and-are-listed-among-fallbacks-if-production-fails-it-seems-but-check-to-be-sure-anyways-etc).Thanks to the increase in AI efficiency and hammer use due to now using hammer instead of "passing" turns, i have reduced handicap settings a bit more to accomodate that (and so AIs don't go bankrupt, as well as human players having a more even game without excessive penalties too ideally so hopefully for the best in all at least to me). AI should be quite a bit stronger thanks to this change. Update: added offense only and defense only alternate logic (fallback to overall units if none available) as i have found/guessed this is where most excess longbowmen came from as they would bypass restrictions in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit`. Update 2: Instead, for watery cities, try to build fallback some of the key water buildings (for food, hammer, gold) rather than unit fallback, as this should/may be economically and also for food/growth more efficient cities than fallback units, plus generally the risk of them being land attacked is not as high. See also [Buildings (non-exhaustive…)](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Custom_Main_Changes_Guide.md#buildings-non-exhaustive-see-sevopedia-orand-xml-for-details) for related buildings info or/and Sevopedia/XML.
