-- some gameplay elements happen at different times, can resarch sooner, build culture sooner, slavery sooner, plantations sooner
-- nerf tech whoreism (if that is a word), which AIs are especially vulnerable to and that humans can abuse, by adding new this technology "Cannot be traded" (`bTrade`) flags to some techs (in a quite limited/focused way), and in tech tree view (tech advisor) as well, in particular for decisive and potentially snowbally ones. Hopefully this makes the game more interesting and strategic, while not preventing tech trading altogether (unless you use the "No Tech Trading" option in custom game if i am not mistaken which disables it entirely), so you can't gain 10 techs in a row with just one tech anymore (at least not for key or decisive ones anymore which is hopefully more balanced as such), but maybe you can still profit a bit from tech trading if you want to play with tech trading enabled. Info added in UI: see [UI (In-game)](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Custom_Main_Changes_Guide.md#ui-in-game) and [UI (Sevopedia reworks & related)](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md#ui-sevopedia-reworks--related) sections.
-- reduce TECH_COST_NOTRADE_MODIFIER from -23 to -20 [GlobalDefines_advc.xml](/Assets/XML/GlobalDefines_advc.xml) for details and or latest updated value), which as reminder according to the base advciv code comment: `<!-- advc.550d: This modifier is applied to tech costs when playing with the "No Tech Trading" option. The effect of the modifier is reduced in the early and late game. Recommended: -23, meaning that tech costs are taken times 100% - 23% = 77%. This multiplier gets further adjusted to the tech era and map size. Use 0 for BtS behavior (tech costs unaffected by the "No Tech Trading" option). -->`
-- game pace advances exponentially: later eras have relatively fewer techs (except future era that has more techs) and unit types. This avoids the hassle of a long and tedious endgame, but is also realistic as most of history in the amount of years, happened in the past. Historically, bronze Age alone was longer than most of recent eras for example, and even more so for Stone Age. There are also less buildings to fasten the end game (but trying to keep just enough techs to make it last long enough) time per turn in minutes/hour played.
-- reworked techs 's FirstFreeUnitClass i mean, (i.e. the techs at which first player to discover the tech gets a free unit class, here a great person type, for example as of now a great spy at the new tech game theory); this is to have greater versatility in strategy and relatively boosting the non-main military path in tech tree if players were to choose/risk to take it and reward it relatively more in this case, with similarly to the corporations great person change only one great person type per tech so that it is balanced and varied as well, generally sooner in tech tree as well so that these have a stronger impact, see xml or tech advisor (i.e. tech tree) or/and sevopedia for details or/and updated values. Note: the future era is the exception: it has it's own GPs.
-- reworked/rebalanced starting techs after our tech tree rework, in particular due to tech_agriculture and tech_the_wheel no longer being starting techs, while tech_pottery now is, with the very nice and tremedous help of chatgpt 5 as well here thanks. Also used this opportunity to balance tech repartition to be equal ideally or as even and balanced as possible (as of now among the 35 playable civilizations, the techs are exactly split at 14 assignments per starting tech, for a total of 70 starting techs (2 per civ), which is very nice if possible). Changes include when culturally relevant for example giving tech_mining to tech_bronze_working or such leaning civilizations when thematically/historically quite/relevant enough. For example we changed the native american starting techs from tech_agriculture and tech_fishing to tech_hunting and tech_mining (goes well with the dog soldier civ-specific unit, and chatgpt 5 gave this rationale if helpful/more exhaustive: "Replace inland Fishing with Mining (abstracts obsidian/flint/quarries). **Dog Soldier** benefits from the Bronze Working line. Adds one OP pair by design; helps identity."). The max occurence of pairs (e.g. tech_hunting + tech_mining is a pair, and tech_mining + tech_hunting is obviously the one and same pair) among all as of now playable 35 civilizations is as of now 4 which is very reasonable and varied enough. I tried to avoid OP combinations but didn't prevent it extra hard either, so gameplay is varied and quite balanced when relevant enough. See for details the as of now list of all civs and their current starting techs with rationale is available there too in [README_Tech_Tree.md#after-most-recent-as-of-now-rework-anyways-etc](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Assets_Rebalancing.md#after-most-recent-as-of-now-rework-anyways-etc) for details (not put here for concision but is synthetic and straightforward unlike this message xd but that i hope is sitll helpful)
-- **Delayed Requirements**: Some techs (e.g. `TECH_MOUNTED_COMBAT`) can optionally be researched fairly early (e.g. at Classical Era) or skipped until significantly later when they become mandatory (e.g. mid-Medieval Era for `TECH_CHIVALRY`). This gives more strategic flexibility and research costs management, as these techs would be cheaper to research later on with a stronger economy, while shorter-term concerns have more room to be addressed sooner if desired.
-- **Hastened Requirements**: Conversely, some techs (e.g. `TECH_CORPORATION`) are available earlier than their column in tech tree would suggest (since their requirements are few), for versatility and diverse strategical choices (thematically, it is also sensical as an alternate reality possibility).
